{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 22, :comments ["<b>And you came near to me, all of you.</b> Rashi explained: 'in disorder' etc., 'the approach at the giving of the Torah was proper' etc. Why does he need to mention now the approach at the giving of the Torah? Rather, because all of this pertains to the matter of rebuke — to say that now I am holding you to account also for the approach at the giving of the Torah. And thus he said to them: At the giving of the Torah I judged you favorably, when I saw children honoring the elders and sending them ahead of themselves; I said, would that this be your heart's disposition all your days. But when you came to send the spies, you came in disorder — children pushing the elders. It was revealed retroactively that even the approach at the giving of the Torah was not proper for the sake of Heaven; and what is more, it is proof that all your desires are acquisitions of the body, whereas the acquisition of the Torah is far from your thoughts. And therefore the children sent the elders ahead of themselves, to say: You go first to receive the Torah, for ״בִּישִׁישִׁים חָכְמָה״, and we are last, for the Torah does not suit us — for you said that once a man has aged, the natural heat within him has already cooled and he no longer yearns so intensely after the desires of this world, as it is written (II Samuel 19:36): ״אִם יִטְעַם עַבְדְּךָ אֵת אֲשֶׁר אֹכַל״; therefore it befits them to engage in wisdom, whereas we shall revel in loves. The day is still long, and it is too much to expect the voice of YHWH in our tents at the time of old age, when our strength is spent. And this was the reason for the honor they showed — that they honored the elders." "And the proof of this is that its end testifies about its beginning: for when you came to send spies in order to inherit the land which lacks nothing of every good in it, you shoved the elders aside with both hands, to say: We go first to enjoy the desires of this world whose source is from the land. And to this day such is the practice in Israel — that specifically in the matter of Torah they honor one another, and sometimes the honor is in order to lighten the burden from himself and to place the yoke of Torah upon someone else. At any rate, when they come to the matter of money, honor departs from Israel: for the children will never honor the elders, to say 'this one is an elder and it is not according to his honor to deal in silver and gold, and it is fitting to give him precedence in some benefit' — but rather whoever is stronger prevails, and there is no wisdom and no understanding that counts for anything against them. And it appears that Rashi inferred all of this from his saying ״וְיַחְפְּרוּ לָנוּ אֶת הָאָרֶץ״ — ״לָנוּ״ is superfluous; rather, this is its meaning: when Moses saw this approach, that the children preceded the elders, he asked them why this was, and they answered him: in order that they may spy out the land for us — for us and not for them, for all the desires of the land are worth nothing except to us. And it is inferred from this that specifically the land is for us, but the acquisition of the Torah is for them. From here I understood from their words that even the approach at the giving of the Torah was not proper. And as for what is written ״וַיִּיטַב בְּעֵינַי הַדָּבָר״ — he said: behold, were it to be according to your words, the land would be yours, and to me and to all the elders my contemporaries the Torah would be ours as an inheritance — for this division is certainly just in my eyes and I am prepared to accept it; but in any case you are guilty — both for casting off the yoke of Torah from upon yourselves, and for being so avidly drawn after the acquisition of money." "Another interpretation: since you said ״וְיָשִׁיבוּ אוֹתָנוּ דָּבָר״ — ״וַיִּיטַב בְּעֵינַי הַדָּבָר״ that they would bring back word, for I was confident in the promise of HaShem, blessed be He, that they would report ״טוֹבָה הָאָרֶץ״; and before I heard their answer, the matter that they would ultimately report was pleasing in my eyes. But not all of their intentions were pleasing in my eyes — neither the matter of sending the spies, nor the improper approach. Therefore he spelled out the matter. And from this improper approach it appears to me possible to resolve what our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Ḥagigah 14a): eighteen curses did Isaiah pronounce upon Israel, and his mind was not set at rest until he said: ״יִרְהֲבוּ הַנַּעַר בַּזָּקֵן״. Now were the prophets rejoicing at their misfortune, Heaven forbid, that he said his mind was set at rest? Rather, this is the meaning: throughout all the curses, his heart burned within him from the burning fire that the doubt kindled in him — to know why this had happened and on account of what did YHWH make their blows so wondrous, and what particular sin had brought all this upon them. And his mind was not set at rest for lack of his heart's telling him from the womb whence all these afflictions had issued — until HaShem, blessed be He, said to him: ״יִרְהֲבוּ הַנַּעַר בַּזָּקֵן״; then it became known to him that all these punishments were drawn from the sin of the spies, that the children were pushing the elders, and by this they established weeping for generations. And to this day we have not purified ourselves from this evil trait — was it not what destroyed both Temples, and its hand is still stretched out until YHWH looks down and sees."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:11:17.878523129Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}